 "example": true,
 "note": "FORMAT EXAMPLE for LONGFORM.md — shows every long-form building block in about two minutes. Facts are evergreen EIA figures (2024). Never publish this file; real episodes are 8–12 minutes and researched on the day.",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {"id": "hook", "text": "Twenty percent of the world's oil passes through a gap just twenty-one miles wide. What happens when it closes?"},
  {"id": "promise", "text": "In the next few minutes: why this strait matters more than any other, who depends on it most, and the escape routes the Gulf has built. Stay to the end for the one number traders worry about most."},
  {"id": "ch1", "pauseBefore": 0.4, "text": "The Strait of Hormuz sits between Iran and Oman. At its narrowest it is about twenty-one miles wide, according to the U.S. Energy Information Administration. But tankers can't use all of it. Ships move in two lanes, each only two miles wide, one in and one out. Every day, roughly twenty million barrels of oil flow through. That is about a fifth of everything the world burns."},
  {"id": "ch2", "pauseBefore": 0.4, "text": "Most of that oil does not go to Europe or America. The EIA estimates that more than eighty percent heads to Asia. China, India, Japan and South Korea are the biggest buyers. For Saudi Arabia, Iraq, Kuwait and the Emirates, Hormuz is the front door to their customers. Qatar's gas tankers have no other route to the ocean at all."},
  {"id": "ch3", "pauseBefore": 0.4, "text": "So what if the strait closes? The Gulf has built two pipelines around it. Saudi Arabia can pump crude west to Yanbu on the Red Sea. The Emirates can send oil to Fujairah, outside the strait. Together, the EIA estimates about two point six million barrels a day of unused capacity. That is the number traders worry about. It covers barely an eighth of what normally sails through Hormuz."},
  {"id": "next", "pauseBefore": 0.4, "text": "Next, we follow the tankers that tried the other way out, through the Red Sea. If you want to know why that route is just as dangerous, this is the video to watch next."}
 ],
 "sections": [
